## Problem

The `claude-p` backend drives the interactive `claude` TUI under a PTY. In an **untrusted directory** (a fresh worktree, a not-yet-opened repo) `claude` blocks on its *"Quick safety check / Do you trust this folder?"* gate, and `claude-p`'s PTY auto-accept of that gate is **non-deterministic** — so the `claude`/`opus` panel member intermittently dies with `workspace_trust_blocked` and the review panel silently drops from three models to two.

The bypass routes are worse: `--permission-mode bypassPermissions` and `--dangerously-skip-permissions` each raise their **own** un-dismissable safety gate the PTY layer can't answer.

## Fix

Before spawning `claude-p`, seed Claude Code's workspace-trust flag for the review `cwd` directly in `~/.claude.json`, keyed by the **resolved real path** (`claude` canonicalises `cwd` — `/tmp` → `/private/tmp`, symlinked worktrees — so a raw-path key silently misses; that was the original root cause). The deterministic `dontAsk` permission mode is **kept**; no permissions or tools are granted, only the trust + onboarding flags.

This is a **deliberate, persistent** edit to the user's global config (review is configured to auto-trust the directory it reviews); a later interactive `claude` session in the same dir inherits the trust. Scoped to the trust flag only.

Best-effort and conservative:
- early-returns if `~/.claude.json` is absent (never fabricates a config);
- **skips the write entirely when already trusted** (common case → no write, no race window vs a concurrent `claude` session);
- forces **both** `hasTrustDialogAccepted` and `hasCompletedProjectOnboarding` true (a prior blocked attempt can leave onboarding=false, which still gates);
- only touches the one project entry; preserves all other state;
- serialises review's own writers with an `flock` on a sidecar lock file;
- writes via same-dir tempfile + atomic `os.replace` (no half-write is ever seen);
- resolves the `claude-p` binary **before** touching trust, so a missing binary raises without mutating the config;
- lazy-imports `fcntl` inside the helper (POSIX-only) so importing `reviewlib` still works on non-POSIX platforms;
- any error (missing/locked/non-JSON config) silently degrades to the old behaviour.

## Tests

- New `tests/test_workspace_trust.py` — 8 cases (seed / idempotent / realpath-key / missing-config / garbage-config / preserve-others / force-onboarding / missing-binary), all with `HOME` isolated to a temp dir so the real config is never touched.
- `test_streaming.py` stubs the helper so its claude-backend test can't mutate the real config.
- `smoke.sh` runs the new suite.

Two `codex` self-review rounds done; all findings addressed (locking, test pollution, helper/`_which` ordering, lazy `fcntl`, force-onboarding, missing smoke entry).

